{% set local_problem %}
<p>Decatur's tree coverage is beautiful — until it ends up in your gutters. Historic oaks in <a href="/recent-projects/">Albany and downtown</a>, pines throughout Point Mallard area, maples and sweetgums in established neighborhoods. They all shed constantly, and it all ends up on your roof.</p>

<p>But Decatur has an extra challenge: river debris. The Tennessee River and Wheeler Lake create an ecosystem of organic matter that goes airborne during storms and windy days. Leaves from miles away, seed pods, pollen, fine particulates — they all settle on Decatur rooftops. You're not just dealing with your trees. You're dealing with the river's trees too.</p>

<p>In Decatur's humidity, that debris doesn't sit there harmlessly. It decomposes into thick composted sludge within weeks. Wet, heavy, nasty stuff that's harder to clear than dry leaves. Standing moisture accelerates gutter corrosion. Creates mosquito breeding habitat. And when gutters overflow during storms, that water goes straight toward foundations already dealing with high groundwater.</p>

<p>Many Decatur homeowners end up in an endless cycle: clean gutters, wait 2-3 months, clean again. At $150-200 per professional cleaning, that's $600-800 annually. Do that for 3 years and you've spent more than quality guards cost. Gutter guards break the cycle permanently.</p>

<p><strong>Our strong opinion:</strong> Don't buy foam gutter inserts. We've pulled thousands of feet of failed foam out of <a href="/recent-projects/">Decatur gutters</a>. The humidity here destroys them — they decompose, trap debris, and become part of the clog within 18-24 months. Spend the money on micro-mesh or don't bother at all.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are protective covers that fit over or inside your gutters. They let water through while blocking leaves, needles, twigs, and the river-blown organics that plague Decatur homes. Simple concept — but product selection matters enormously.</p>

<p><strong>What works in Decatur:</strong> Micro-mesh guards with surgical-grade stainless steel mesh. The holes are tiny — around 50 microns — blocking even fine pine needles and river particulates. Water sheets through; debris stays on top and blows or washes off.</p>

<p><strong>What doesn't work here:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Foam inserts — they decompose in humidity and become part of the clog (we've seen this hundreds of times)</li>
<li>Plastic snap-in screens — they warp in Alabama heat and don't block fine debris</li>
<li>Brush-style guards — pine needles embed in the bristles and are nearly impossible to clean out</li>
</ul>

<p>We also consider Decatur's humidity when recommending specific products. Some guard designs trap moisture underneath, accelerating fascia rot. We recommend systems that allow airflow while blocking debris — keeping your gutters and the wood behind them healthy.</p>

<p><strong>The realistic expectation:</strong> Quality guards reduce cleaning needs by 90%+. You might brush surface debris off the top once a year. That's not the same as "never clean again," but it's a lot better than scooping decomposed sludge out of your gutters four times a year.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed guards on hundreds of <a href="/recent-projects/">Decatur homes</a> and learned exactly which products survive river-town conditions. We're not pushing the most expensive option or whatever has the best margin. We recommend what actually works here — which often isn't what works in drier climates.</p>

<p>As local contractors serving Morgan County for 20+ years, we stand behind the work. If something isn't performing, we're 25 miles away and we'll make it right.</p>

<p><strong>Warning:</strong> Be skeptical of gutter guard companies offering "lifetime" cleaning guarantees. Read the fine print. Most exclude "maintenance debris" — defined broadly enough to cover almost everything. We're upfront: guards reduce cleaning 90%+, not 100%. Occasional surface maintenance happens. That's the honest reality.</p>

<p><strong>Our recommendation:</strong> For most Decatur homes with mixed tree coverage and river debris exposure, micro-mesh guards are worth the investment. They handle everything this environment throws at them. If you have light tree coverage and minimal debris, screens might suffice at lower cost. We'll assess your actual situation, not sell you more than you need.</p>
{% endset %}

